Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary Midwestern drama, naturalistic faces, fabric, weather, and locations.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A worn fifth-floor apartment overlooks Milwaukee. NARRATOR, a lean young man in a pizza-parlor jacket, carries groceries beside MA, a tired older woman with soft brown hair and an afghan over her knees.

(SOUND: radiator hisses, game-show applause from the television)

NARRATOR V.O.
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

NICK, broad-shouldered and powerful, wears a brown suede jacket over a fitted shirt. Reddish-brown hair falls untamed around scars on his chin and left eyebrow as lifters clear his path.

(SOUND: iron plates clank, a weight bar thuds to rubber flooring)

NARRATOR V.O.
His face looked like a puzzle held together with glue-gun stuff. He wasn’t ugly. He had a few brawls over the years.

NICK

You got to keep an eye on Ma.

NARRATOR

I do.

NICK

She shouldn’t be sleeping all day. That’s how old people go downhill.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Twelve-year-old NICK stands before MRS. ENGEL’S shattered kitchen window. Young NARRATOR and MARK CONNER stare at a kickball amid dented garbage-can-lid bases.

(SOUND: glass settles onto linoleum, children’s sneakers scrape gravel)

NICK

I did it.

MRS. ENGEL

I’m calling your mother.

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick gave Ma cash for the window a week later. He never told her it was really Mark.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

NARRATOR wheels his bicycle toward work. SIONNA stumbles on the cement and catches herself against him. She is slender and pale, with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a fish-symbol necklace.

(SOUND: bicycle chain ticks, traffic washes past)

NARRATOR

Are you okay?

SIONNA

Yes.

NARRATOR

I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA

Sionna.

NARRATOR

Sionna? That’s different.

SIONNA

My parents named me after an Irish goddess.

NARRATOR

Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA

No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

NARRATOR V.O.
Her eyes were the blue of the sky before the sun comes up.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA lines medicine bottles across the kitchen counter, then searches among them with trembling hands. NARRATOR watches from the doorway, Sionna’s phone number folded in his palm.

(SOUND: pill bottles click against the counter, clock ticks)

MA

I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today?

NARRATOR

Your pills are right in front of you, Ma.

MA

Oh. Right. I don’t know what got into me.

NARRATOR V.O.
Her eyes looked glassy and empty, like she didn’t know who I was.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

NICK enters through smoke and Packers-game light, his heavy boots loud on the floor. NARRATOR sits alone with a beer, shoulders rounded.

(SOUND: football announcer on television, pool balls crack together)

NICK

What’s goin’ on?

NARRATOR

Ma got confused today.

NICK

Why don’t you take her to the store or something?

NARRATOR

I just needed to get away from her.

NICK

Do something before we have to pay for a nursing home.

NARRATOR V.O.
Sometimes I really hated Nick inside. I never said it.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

NARRATOR enters a vast old reading room of wood floors, hanging lamps, and tall shelves. LIBRARIAN slides a temporary card across the desk.

(SOUND: pages turn, distant footsteps cross old floorboards)

NARRATOR

How do I take a book home from here?

LIBRARIAN

Do you have a library card?

NARRATOR

No.

LIBRARIAN

Fill this out. Your plastic card will be mailed within the week.

NARRATOR V.O.
I found classic literature and took home Wuthering Heights.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

SIONNA sits at a small back table in a loose green hooded top, backpack at her feet. NARRATOR approaches with a coffee, clutching his library book.

(SOUND: espresso machine steams, students murmur around them)

SIONNA

What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR

Wuthering Heights. It’s about this guy and girl who grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA

The environment is like its people. Wild, unpredictable, infertile.

SIONNA

Heathcliff’s love for Catherine never changes. In the end, that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR V.O.
Something inside me was coming awake.

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Wuthering Heights lies open beside NARRATOR’s bed. Moonlight catches his face as he sleeps.

(SOUND: winter wind taps the window, bedsprings creak)

EXT. ANCIENT FOREST - DAY

NARRATOR, now in a red embroidered tunic and brown hose, rides behind SIONNA on a dappled mare. Her long brown hair reaches her waist as they enter a sunlit field.

(SOUND: horse hooves soften in grass, insects buzz in warm air)

NARRATOR V.O.
I knew her. It was Sionna, but in a different place with old clothes on.

NARRATOR helps SIONNA down. They embrace and kiss.

(MUSIC: intimate strings rise beneath the field’s wind)

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR bolts upright, drenched in sweat.

(SOUND: his breath pounds in the cold room, wind rattles the glass)

NARRATOR

Hold!

NARRATOR V.O.
What the hell was that?

INT. YOGA STUDIO - EVENING

NARRATOR struggles on a purple mat, shirt tucked into his shorts. SIONNA moves steadily nearby, calm and balanced.

(SOUND: measured breathing, floorboards give under shifting feet)

SIONNA

Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

NARRATOR

My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA

That’s why they call it practice.

NARRATOR smiles despite himself.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NICK, face red and fists clenched, stands over NARRATOR. MA sits small in her chair, staring at the television. A blue weekly pill organizer rests on the table.

(SOUND: television audience laughs, apartment door slams in the hallway)

NICK

Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass.

NARRATOR

I totally forgot. I was with someone.

NICK

A girl? That’s worth all this?

NARRATOR

I said I was sorry.

NICK turns and leaves.

(SOUND: the front door slams, MA’s spoon trembles against a cup)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

MA lies in a hospital bed. NARRATOR dozes in a chair beside her while SIONNA, now in a pale cardigan, holds his hand.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeps, oxygen hums through the room)

NARRATOR V.O.
They kept Ma three days. Her blood pressure was dangerously high. They said she had dementia.

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT

NARRATOR, armored and mud-streaked, stands among fallen soldiers. NICK charges through smoke in dark armor, wild reddish hair blazing under firelight.

(SOUND: swords clash, men cry out, wind whips battle flags)

NARRATOR

Nick! Thank God you’re here!

NICK

That you are, little brother.

NICK drives a long knife into NARRATOR’s chest.

(SOUND: blade enters, battle noise abruptly muffles)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR wakes gasping beside MA’s bed.

(SOUND: heart monitor quickens, MA’s blanket rustles)

NARRATOR V.O.
Why would Nick want to kill me? It was only a dream. Still, betrayal stayed with me all day.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

NICK drinks with BOB and SAMMY near the pool tables. NARRATOR sits apart, older now in a college sweatshirt, watching Nick ridicule Sionna.

(SOUND: heavy rock music, glasses clink, cues strike balls)

NICK

He’s got the perfect girlfriend. She has him wrapped right now.

NARRATOR

You three wouldn’t recognize class and intelligence if it jumped up and bit your ass.

NICK spots LINDSEY with her new boyfriend across the room. His jaw tightens.

NARRATOR

Leave it, man. She can see whoever she wants.

NICK

Did I ask your opinion?

NICK advances toward LINDSEY’S BOYFRIEND. NARRATOR blocks him.

(SOUND: music lowers beneath a gathering hush, a cue stick drops)

NARRATOR

Stop it! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

NICK swings. His fist catches NARRATOR across the face.

(SOUND: fist cracks, LINDSEY screams)

JOE and his bouncer seize NICK as NARRATOR flees through the bar door.

(SOUND: door bangs open, boots pound wet pavement outside)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR collapses on SIONNA’S green suede sofa. SIONNA strokes his bruised cheek; her light-blue eyes hold steady.

(SOUND: rain taps the duplex windows, a wall clock ticks)

SIONNA

Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR

He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA

No one who really cares about you does that.

NARRATOR

What is my purpose?

SIONNA

Find the true self within you, and honor it.

SIONNA cups his jaw. He rests his head in her lap.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - EVENING

SIONNA serves peach pie to MA. NICK enters in his brown suede jacket, big boots, and untamed reddish-brown hair. He stops when SIONNA offers her hand.

(SOUND: forks scrape plates, television cop show murmurs)

SIONNA

I’m Sionna. You must be Nick.

NICK

Likewise.

NICK

So, I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA

That’s right.

NICK

It’s tough out there. School doesn’t always pay off.

SIONNA

You can’t keep a good woman down, so to speak.

NICK

Women can’t stay home and take care of their kids anymore.

SIONNA

Women bring different ways of thinking to the table. Diversity helps us learn.

MA looks from SIONNA to NICK with quiet wonder.

(SOUND: a spoon slips from MA’s fingers, then clinks on the plate)

NICK leans close to NARRATOR at the door.

NICK

Take care of Ma.

NARRATOR

You know I will.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment is silent. MA’S chair is empty; her afghan lies folded over one arm. NARRATOR presses the blinking answering machine.

(SOUND: answering machine beeps, refrigerator motor hums)

OFFICER DUPUIS V.O.
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

NARRATOR pedals his bicycle hard through city traffic.

(SOUND: bicycle tires hiss on pavement, car horns blare)

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, a large man with graying hair and a thin mustache, sits across from NARRATOR in a cramped office.

(SOUND: phones ring beyond the office door, fluorescent lights buzz)

OFFICER DUPUIS

Your brother and mother were hit broadside last night.

OFFICER DUPUIS

Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene.

NARRATOR grips a paper cup until water spills over his hand.

(SOUND: cup crumples, water drips onto tile)

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick killed her.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NARRATOR stands before MA’S empty chair. SIONNA enters and holds him as his knees fold.

(SOUND: NARRATOR’S sobs fill the room, rain lashes the window)

NARRATOR

Die, you fucking jackass. Die.

SIONNA

Just let it out. We’ll figure it all out.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

NARRATOR drops a handful of dirt onto MA’S light maple casket. SIONNA stands beside him in a dark coat, holding his arm.

(SOUND: dirt strikes wood, bare branches creak in autumn wind)

FATHER JOSEPH

Into your hands, O Lord, we commend Olivia.

NARRATOR V.O.
One day you’re talking to her. The next day you’re putting her in a box.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

NICK lies broken beneath white sheets, bruised yellow and purple, a chest tube at his side. His reddish-brown hair is matted against the pillow. NARRATOR stands at the foot of the bed.

(SOUND: monitor beeps, oxygen hisses, television murmurs)

NICK

I guess I really fucked up, man.

NARRATOR

The mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK

They’re saying vehicular manslaughter.

NARRATOR

Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK

You think I meant to kill Ma?

NARRATOR

Think about it.

NARRATOR turns away.

(SOUND: NICK’s call button clicks repeatedly, monitor continues its steady beep)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

SIONNA’S pale face is pressed against the hallway wall. NICK, in his suede jacket and wild hair, grips her throat with one hand and holds an open switchblade in the other.

(SOUND: rain needles the windows, the gray cat hisses from the kitchen)

NICK

Break it off with him tonight.

SIONNA

Nick, calm down. This has nothing to do with me.

NICK

You came along and confused his stupid brain.

NICK presses the blade near her collarbone.

(SOUND: blade flicks against metal, SIONNA’s breath squeaks)

NICK

No more brother. No more contact. You got that?

NARRATOR bursts through the front door and grabs NICK’S arm.

(SOUND: front door slams the wall, NARRATOR shouts)

NARRATOR

Nick! What the fuck are you doing?

NICK jerks free. The blade cuts SIONNA. Blood stains her shirt as she sinks toward the floor.

(SOUND: SIONNA gasps, the switchblade clatters, sirens rise outside)

NARRATOR presses both hands against SIONNA’S wound.

NARRATOR

Stay with me. Stay with me.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months later, SIONNA sits in a rocking chair, a scar visible near her left shoulder. NARRATOR kneels before her. Packed bags wait by the door.

(SOUND: rocking chair creaks, a distant lawn mower drones)

SIONNA

I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR

You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA

As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you.

NARRATOR watches her walk into the bedroom.

(MUSIC: a single piano note repeats and fades)

EXT. PIKE PLACE MARKET - DAY

Seven years later, PETER OLSEN, now sturdier and well dressed, walks through rainy Seattle. His hair is trimmed, his posture self-possessed. A Tony’s Boy’s Pizza storefront glows beneath a medical building.

(SOUND: market vendors call, gulls cry, rain patters on awnings)

NARRATOR V.O.
I was two people: the child in Wisconsin and the man in Washington.

NARRATOR V.O.
Thanks to Tony, I had my own chain of pizzerias. Tony’s Boy’s Pizza.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

PETER sits on a mat among candles, eyes closed. The room carries citrus and lavender. His chest rises and settles.

(SOUND: synchronized breathing, candle wicks crackle)

INSTRUCTOR

What are you grateful for?

PETER V.O.
I couldn’t stop feeling the void left by Sionna, and the resentfulness toward Nick.

EXT. DREAM ROAD - DAY

PETER walks a radiant green road beside a dark-skinned SAGE in flowing white cloth. A discarded pizza box sits in the grass.

(SOUND: birdsong, soft wind through tall grass)

PETER

It’s terrible. It’s an eyesore.

SAGE

Stop judging things. Things are neither good nor bad.

SAGE

Start living the truth.

PETER wakes in his Seattle apartment beside BOZO, an orange-and-white cat.

(SOUND: BOZO purrs, Seattle rain taps the glass)

PETER V.O.
I had to find forgiveness.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE VISITING ROOM - DAY

PETER sits behind thick plexiglass, phone in hand. NICK enters in an orange prison uniform. He is thinner now; gray touches his hair, and the old scars remain under tired eyes.

(SOUND: locked doors buzz, phones murmur through glass)

NICK

Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER

Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK

It’s not a cakewalk. I got my routine down.

PETER

I need you to listen.

NICK

Okay, man.

PETER

You killed Ma in a drunk driving accident.

PETER

You held me down my entire life so you could have the exciting life.

PETER

You tried to kill the love of my life. You’re an asshole, Nick.

NICK lowers his eyes.

(SOUND: a guard’s keys jangle, another visitor weeps nearby)

PETER

But I forgive you.

PETER

It sets me free from anger, regret, and your control over me forever.

NICK’S face crumples. Tears slip down his cheeks.

(SOUND: tears tap the Formica counter, prison intercom crackles)

NICK

I’m so sorry.

NICK

You’re more than I ever was. You deserve to live out there.

PETER

I still love you for being my brother.

NICK

What happened with you?

PETER

I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’ll be expanding.

PETER

You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK

That’s fucking amazing. I’m proud of you, man.

GUARD

Time’s up.

PETER hangs up. NICK presses his palm to the glass before officers lead him away.

(SOUND: receiver clicks into place, doors lock behind NICK)

INT. SEATTLE AIRPORT GATE - DAY

PETER sits with tea and mixed nuts, watching planes move through rain beyond the windows. His boarding pass rests in his hand.

(SOUND: boarding announcements echo, jet engines rumble beyond glass)

PETER V.O.
The weight of thirty years was gone.

PETER looks down at the printed name on his boarding pass.

PETER

President Peter Olsen.

PETER smiles, rises, and joins the boarding line.

FADE OUT.

THE END
